<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mirzhakip Dulatov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a>–<a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mirzhakyp Dulatuly</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mirzhakip Dulatov was a Kazakh poet, journalist and politician, a founder of the Alash party. His poetry collection Oyan, qazaq! ('Wake up, Kazakh!', 1909) was a landmark of the Kazakh national awakening, and he co-edited the newspaper Qazaq with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhmet-baitursynov">Akhmet Baitursynov</a>. Arrested by the Soviet authorities in 1928, he died in a labour camp in 1935.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mirzhakip-dulatov.jpg" alt="Mirzhakip Dulatov" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dulatov_Mirjakip.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dulatuly was born on 25 November 1885 in the village of Sarikopa in Turgay Oblast, into a Muslim family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyn">Argyn</a> tribe of the Middle jüz. He lost his mother Demesh at the age of two and his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dulat-tribe">Dulat</a> at twelve. After the village school he entered a Kazakh-Russian school in 1897 and qualified as a village teacher in 1902. In 1904 he met Ahmet Baitursynuly and Älihan Bökeihan at Karkaraly, and under their influence came to hold anti-colonial, anti-Russian views.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1907 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St. Petersburg</a>, became a delegate of the Constitutional Democratic Party and published his first poem, Jastarğa (To the Youth), in the journal Serke, which ran to only one issue. Oyan! Qazaq was confiscated as soon as it appeared in 1909 and was republished in 1911. His novel Baqytsyz Jamal (Jamal the Unfortunate, 1910), the story of a Kazakh girl who falls victim to patriarchal customs, was the first novel of modern Kazakh literature. Under surveillance he could hold no steady job, and after his arrest at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semey">Semey</a> in 1911 he served a year and a half in prison.</p><p>He then wrote for the Kazakh periodicals Ayqap and Qazaq, criticising the conditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> under imperial rule, and published the collection Azamat (Citizen) in 1913. As one of the leaders of the Alash Orda government he sided with the Whites against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> from December 1917 to May 1919. When Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-kolchak">Aleksandr Kolchak</a> turned down their requests for help, the Alash leaders realigned with the Bolsheviks in hope of autonomy, and Dulatuly worked as an editor and teacher under the new government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Arrested on charges of nationalism in 1928, he died in the Solovki labour camp in 1935. He was rehabilitated after his death in 1988 and is considered a pioneer of modern Kazakh literature and a leader of Kazakh nationalism. He also wrote under the pen names Madiyar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyn">Argyn</a>, and the 1993 film Return of Mir-Yakub, made by the director Kalila Umarov, is dedicated to him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mirjaqip Dulatuli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Alash party was founded by Kazakh intellectuals including Alikhan Bukeikhanov, Akhmet Baitursynov and <b>Mirzhakip Dulatov</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/alash-autonomy">Alash Autonomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Turgaysky Uyezd. Died in Solovetsky Islands, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=65.02,35.71&amp;year=1917" aria-label="Solovetsky Islands on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5260 35 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Mirzhakip Dulatov"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5710 305h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5710 305h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=65.02,35.71&amp;year=1917">What was here in 1917? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
